&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1020&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td align=3D&quot;right&quot;&gt;25000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3200&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td align=3D&quot;right&quot;&gt;10000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3201&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td align=3D&quot;right&quot;&gt;8000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3202&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td align=3D&quot;right&quot;&gt;7000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;manual_group_entry&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;
Can be used to create multiple one line bookings in one group entry. This m=
eans that the bookings will have the same &lt;code&gt;reference_nr&lt;/code=
&gt; but can differ in accounts, currencies, etc.
The following example shows how two received bank transaction can be booked=
